{-
   What an `alias` refuses.

   The cycle is the one a transparent alias can actually make: it stands for what it names, so a
   pair that name each other stands for nothing. Reported where the walk sees it rather than by
   running out of stack, which is the same three-colour walk `Reject.Rank.yana` uses for defaults.

   A **qualified** alias cannot make one, and that is not an omission here: it is a record - see
   `Resolver::newtypeRecord` - so `alias qualified A = B` naming a `B` that is `alias qualified B = A`
   is two types each holding the other, which is a record cycle and reported as one. `Newtype.yana`
   is what a qualified alias does.

   A `deriving` clause on a plain alias needs no refusal here at all - the parser admits one only on
   the qualified form, because a plain alias names the same type as its target and that type already
   has whatever instances it has.
-}

alias Loop = Ring
alias Ring = Loop

fn main() -> I32 = 0
